<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- A choice between a few options, on a light surface.

         WHY THIS EXISTS. The product's only RadioButton style is the one the rail wears: light
         text for a navy background. Worn on a white screen, an unselected option is white on
         white - invisible until clicked. Seen on screen 2026-08-20 on the Assets level control,
         where two of the three options could not be read.

         Keyed rather than implicit, and the rail's style is left alone: the rail has been looked
         at and is right, and restyling it to fix a different screen is how a reviewed thing
         quietly breaks.

         LABEL CONTENT CAN BE TEXT OR A TITLE WITH A DESCRIPTION. Present the content itself:
         converting it to Text loses the wizard's composed labels.

         WHAT WAS HERE BEFORE. This held a pill-shaped style, ChoiceButton, drawing each option as
         a filled rectangle. It went on 2026-09-03 with its last user. The level control moved into
         the 300-pixel tree column, where three pills in a row clipped the third off the screen
         entirely, and the owner asked for radio buttons: a filled pill states the answer but not
         the question, where a ring and a dot say "pick exactly one" without the operator having to
         hover to find out. Deleted rather than kept, because a style nothing wears is one more
         thing the next reader has to decide about. -->

    <!-- One option of a choice: a ring, a dot when it is the answer, and its label.

         STACKS RATHER THAN SITTING IN A ROW, which is what the tree column needs.

         THE BRUSHES INSIDE THE TEMPLATE ARE DYNAMIC, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT COSMETIC. A
         StaticResource in a template's visual tree is resolved when the template is instantiated,
         against the lookup chain of the element wearing it - control, view, application, theme -
         and NOT against this dictionary's siblings. It therefore worked only because
         Application.Resources happens to be the last stop, and failed outright the first time this
         style was worn by a view loaded without an application: XamlParseException, "Cannot find
         resource named 'BorderBrush'". Nothing renders differently; the lookup is simply made in
         the place it was always being made from.

         THE SAME SHAPE IS STILL PRESENT ELSEWHERE, in the trigger setters of Controls.xaml and
         Tabs.xaml. Those fire only on hover or focus, so nothing has exercised them outside a
         running application yet. Left alone here rather than swept up: a reviewed visual file is
         not the place for an unrequested pass. Recorded as a finding instead. -->

    <Style x:Key="ChoiceRadio" TargetType="RadioButton">
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,4,0,0" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="RadioButton">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Background="Transparent">
                        <Grid Width="16" Height="16" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,8,0">
                            <Ellipse x:Name="Ring"
                                     Stroke="{DynamicResource BorderBrush}"
                                     StrokeThickness="1"
                                     Fill="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBackground}" />
                            <Ellipse x:Name="Dot"
                                     Width="8" Height="8"
                                     Fill="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}"
                                     Visibility="Collapsed" />
                        </Grid>
                        <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                                          ContentSource="Content"
                                          VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                          TextElement.Foreground="{DynamicResource PrimaryTextBrush}" />
                    </StackPanel>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Ring" Property="Stroke"
                                    Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Dot" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Ring" Property="Stroke"
                                    Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
